# `is_after_variable`, the parameter that is not carried - and the reason this
# seating is a `marrow` and not a `fence`.
#
# `"$b[0]-$c->d!"` reads the same bytes two ways depending only on which
# terminal the parse state named. Immediately behind `$b`, a `[` opens a
# subscript and a `->` opens a member access; three bytes later, behind
# nothing, the `-` of `-$c` is ordinary content. Nothing in the scanner
# remembers that a variable just ended: the parser asks for
# `encapsed_string_chars_after_variable` in one state and
# `encapsed_string_chars` in the other, and the distinction arrives with the
# question.
#
# So this file fails in BOTH directions of the obvious mistake. A walk that
# always stopped at `[` would cut `!` wrong and never produce the `-` content
# run; a walk that never stopped would swallow `[0]` into the body and lose
# `subscript_expression` and `member_access_expression` entirely - while still
# producing an `encapsed_string` and still parsing whole. Checking for a tree
# would pass it. The spans are what catch it.
#
# Claims are tree-sitter's tree over these bytes.
roots 1
mends 0
holds encapsed_string
holds subscript_expression
holds member_access_expression
lacks text
spans subscript_expression 12 17
spans member_access_expression 18 23
spans string_content 17 18
